FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] bootstrap/intern_selftest.rb:107 — The probe prints `distinct_buckets`, `max_chain`, `spread_growth`, `ordinary_a_bucket` and `ordinary_b_bucket`, but no Rust test ever reads any of those keys (grep over `tests/bootstrap_selfhost_test.rs` finds zero references; the five tests at lines 2208–2408 assert only repeat growth, the engineered collision, empty/10KB names, the type error, and stage1/stage2 byte equality). A degenerate `intern_hash` that always returns 0 would put every name in one bucket and still pass all five tests: `repeat_growth` stays 1, the collision is trivially found at `collision-1`, duplicate lookups still resolve within the single chain, and pool/emission order is unchanged. So the phase's central property — that the hash actually distributes names so lookups walk a bucket instead of the whole table — is unverified, and the comment at intern_selftest.rb:82-85 claiming those counts are what notices a linear scan is false. Fix: add assertions in the test suite such as `intern_number("distinct_buckets") >= 200`, `intern_number("max_chain") <= 5`, and `intern_report("ordinary_a_bucket") != intern_report("ordinary_b_bucket")`.
2. [MINOR] bootstrap/compiler.rb:2683 — `G_INTERN_COUNT` is test-only instrumentation (declared at line 60, only ever read by `bootstrap/intern_selftest.rb`) left inside the shipped compiler, contradicting the stated goal that the compiler not carry test-only code and adding a global plus an increment to every new name. The probe could measure growth with `length(G_INTERN)` instead. Fix: remove `G_INTERN_COUNT` and its increment, and use `length(G_INTERN)` in the fragment.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 2
